WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.480
"How Much Caffeine Is
Safe During Pregnancy?"

00:00:06.595 --> 00:00:09.280
Is caffeine safe
during pregnancy?

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:13.280
Pregnancy greatly reduces our
ability to metabolize caffeine.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.040
For example, in a typical adult,

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:17.840
caffeine blood levels
fall by about half,

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:21.000
also known as the "half-life",
in five hours.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.840
As you reach mid-pregnancy,
that time doubles,

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:26.120
and by the time you reach
the third trimester,

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:28.480
it can take 15 hours, or more,

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:31.160
to reduce caffeine
to a similar level.

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:34.320
At the same time, fatigue is one
of the most common complaints

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:35.800
of pregnant women.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:37.560
Couldn't a little
extra energy boost

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:40.960
from slowly metabolizing
caffeine be a good thing?

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:44.360
Unfortunately, caffeine readily
passes through the placenta,

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:47.440
and if you think expecting moms
metabolize slowly,

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:50.760
babies have even less capacity
to metabolize caffeine,

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:55.240
with a half-life of 80 hours;
that's more than three days!

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:56.640
But, to what effect?

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:01.040
In pregnancy,
is caffeine a friend or foe?

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:02.160
Respected authorities,

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:04.280
including the
World Health Organization,

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:06.040
the European
Food Safety Authority,

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:08.920
and The American College
of Obstetrics and Gynecology

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:12.600
have guidelines for a
"positive pregnancy experience,"

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:14.680
including restricting
caffeine intake

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:16.480
to reduce the risk
of pregnancy loss

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:18.720
and low birth weights.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:20.800
Higher caffeine intake
pre-pregnancy

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:23.880
is associated with higher risk
of miscarriages,

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:26.440
and higher intake
during pregnancy

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:28.520
is associated
with low birth weight,

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:31.560
so the World Health Organization
suggests reducing consumption

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:35.800
to less than 300 mg
of caffeine a day,

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:37.960
whereas the
European Food Safety Authority

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:41.720
suggests 200 mg per day
may be the safety limit,

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:46.640
in agreement with The American College
of Obstetrics and Gynecology.

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:48.440
That comes out to about
two cups (480 ml)

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:50.200
of brewed coffee a day,

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:52.880
assuming that's
your sole caffeine source.

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:54.880
Since those guidelines
were issued,

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:56.440
this study of 2,000 moms

00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:59.960
found that even just a half cup
of coffee (120 ml) or so a day

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:02.320
was associated with
a lower birth weight

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:04.160
by about two ounces (60 g),

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:08.400
which suggests that exposure to
even less than 100 mg of caffeine

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:11.440
may be associated with
decreased fetal growth.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:14.880
The question is,
is it cause and effect?

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.960
Is there something
about caffeine that's doing it,

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:20.600
or something about
caffeine drinkers?

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:23.960
You can't know unless
you put it to the test.

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:25.080
Or, in this case,

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:27.600
do a Mendelian
randomization study,

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:31.080
which is kind of a proxy for
a randomized controlled trial.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:33.400
Since due to the luck
of the draw with genes,

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:35.080
some people randomly
have a tendency

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:39.520
to drink more or less coffee
throughout their life.

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:41.320
And it did not find
a strong relationship

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:44.040
between genetic determinants
of coffee consumption

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:45.880
and these adverse outcomes,

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:50.560
so it isn't clear if the links
that have been found are causal.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:53.200
There appears to have only been
one randomized controlled trial

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:54.920
that tried to test it.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:57.640
More than a thousand
coffee-drinking pregnant women

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:00.280
were randomized
to caffeinated coffee or decaf

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:03.680
at an average of four months
into their pregnancies.

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:05.080
To improve compliance,

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:08.160
they were just asked
to switch to the provided coffee

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:11.360
without being prescribed
a specific caffeine intake.

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:13.320
But, thanks to
the complimentary coffee,

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:14.560
the women in the decaf group

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:18.920
ended up consuming 200 fewer mg
of caffeine a day

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:21.480
than those in the
caffeinated coffee group.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:24.440
So, in the end, the decaf group
complied with the recommendation

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:28.440
of getting less than
200 to 300 mg of caffeine a day,

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:29.760
and the caffeine group did not.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:32.480
Was there any difference
in birth weight

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:35.120
or length of pregnancy
between the two?

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:36.600
No.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.880
Is it possible the women
were all getting more

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:41.440
than some threshold amount?

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:43.000
We don't know.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:44.640
I'm sympathetic
to the view that says,

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:47.600
"Look, the need for caution
is compelling."

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:49.760
So, we should err
on the side of caution,

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:51.680
and tell women
contemplating pregnancy,

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:54.880
or who are pregnant,
to avoid it completely.

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:56.560
But women should realize
that the evidence

00:03:56.560 --> 00:03:58.800
implicating caffeine
in birth outcomes

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:00.316
is far from settled.

